lesson.body:
Linear search checks a list one element at a time, from the start, until it finds the target or runs out of elements. That approach works on any list, sorted or not, but it can require looking at every single element in the worst case. Binary search takes a completely different strategy, and it can only be used under one important condition: the list must already be sorted. This precondition matters because sortedness is exactly what makes the strategy trustworthy: if you know the list is in order, comparing the target to the middle element tells you which half of the list the target could possibly be in, and lets you throw the other half away entirely.

Here is the idea in words. Look at the middle element of the list. If it equals the target, you are done. If the target is smaller than the middle element, the target (if it exists at all) must be somewhere in the left half, because the list is sorted, so you can ignore the right half completely. If the target is larger, the same logic applies to the right half. Either way, you have eliminated half of the remaining elements with a single comparison, and you repeat the process on whichever half is left.

This description is naturally recursive: the recursive case is "search a smaller sorted slice using the exact same procedure," and the base case is "the slice is empty, meaning the target is not present," or "the middle element is the target, meaning you found it." Here is binary search written that way in Python:

def binary_search(arr, target, low=0, high=None):
    if high is None:
        high = len(arr) - 1
    if low > high:
        return -1
    mid = (low + high) // 2
    if arr[mid] == target:
        return mid
    elif target < arr[mid]:
        return binary_search(arr, target, low, mid - 1)
    else:
        return binary_search(arr, target, mid + 1, high)

Trace this by hand on the sorted list arr = [2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 45, 56, 72, 91] (indices 0 through 10), searching for target = 45.

Step 1: low = 0, high = 10, so mid = (0 + 10) // 2 = 5. arr[5] is 23. Since 45 > 23, the target must be in the right half, so the next call searches with low = 6, high = 10.

Step 2: low = 6, high = 10, so mid = (6 + 10) // 2 = 8. arr[8] is 56. Since 45 < 56, the next call searches with low = 6, high = 7.

Step 3: low = 6, high = 7, so mid = (6 + 7) // 2 = 6. arr[6] is 38. Since 45 > 38, the next call searches with low = 7, high = 7.

Step 4: low = 7, high = 7, so mid = 7. arr[7] is 45, which matches the target, so the function returns 7.

In four comparisons, binary search located the target in an 11-element list. Notice how each step roughly halves the number of candidates: 11 elements, then about 5, then about 2, then 1. That halving is the source of binary search's efficiency: after k steps, the search space shrinks to roughly n divided by 2 to the k power, so it only takes about log base 2 of n steps to narrow down to a single element. This is why binary search runs in O(log n) time, in sharp contrast to linear search's O(n). The tradeoff is the precondition: binary search only works because the list is sorted, and if you called it on an unsorted list, the halving logic would no longer guarantee the target lands in the half you kept.
